/*  A module that carries a TYPE, not just members.

    This is the half of the module system that `pennies.cufe` does not show. A book exports
    members — things you reach with `'s`. A module may also carry DECLARATIONS, and that is the
    only way a type crosses a file boundary: a file's top level is private, and an object body
    otherwise takes only `Bind`, `Get` and `Set`.

    ★ **Members are what an object HAS; declarations are what a module CARRIES.** A type is not a
    member — nothing reaches it with `'s`, and no interface can require one — so only a module may
    hold one. An ordinary object that tries is refused, and the message names the fix.

    ★ `between` returns a `span`, so the carried type is in this module's own signatures as well
    as in the caller's. See booking.cufe for the other side: pulling this brings `span` into scope
    by its SHORT name, for the length of the block, and it is gone after the `Done.`

    ⚠ Two modules may each carry a `span` without colliding — the file is what separates them,
    the same way it hides a book's working material.
*/

Define object spans with () and module:

    /// A closed interval on the number line: both edges count as inside.
    Define object span with (the number low, the number high):

        /// How far it reaches.
        Bind number to width:
            Return one's high - one's low.
        Done.

        /// Whether a value falls inside, edges included.
        Bind fact to covers, given (the number value):
            Return value is not less than one's low and value is not greater than one's high.
        Done.
    Done.

    /// Makes a span, putting the edges in order whichever way round they arrive.
    Bind span to between, given (the number one-edge, the number other-edge):
        If one-edge is greater than other-edge, return a new span { the low other-edge, the high one-edge }.
        Return a new span { the low one-edge, the high other-edge }.
    Done.
Done.
